WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.054
Book Three, Proposition Twenty-One.

2
00:00:03.054 --> 00:00:07.946
In a circle, angles in the same segment are equal to one another.

3
00:00:07.946 --> 00:00:15.096
Let A B C D be a circle, and let angles B A D and B E D lie in the same circle segment B A E D.

4
00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:19.010
We are to prove that angle B A D equals angle B E D.

5
00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:27.560
Take the center F of circle A B C D by Three One, and join the radii B F and F D by Postulate One.

6
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:40.950
Because angle B F D is at the center and angle B A D is at the circumference, and both stand on the same base arc B C D, by Three Twenty angle B F D is double angle B A D.

7
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.956
For the exact same reason, angle B F D is also double angle B E D.

8
00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:55.740
Since both inscribed angles are halves of the same central angle B F D, angle B A D equals angle B E D, as was required to prove.

9
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:12.122
As Heath notes, Euclid's direct appeal to Three Twenty assumes a segment greater than a semicircle so the central angle B F D is less than two right angles; Robert Simson extended the proof to all segments by drawing diameter A F C to split the angle into two major segments.

10
00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:20.945
Its converse establishes the fundamental test for concyclic points: any vertices on the same side of a base that subtend equal angles lie on one circle.

11
00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:32.960
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred twenty-four synthesizes six direct rules across fifty ancestor nodes, turning the central-angle ratio of Three Twenty into a direct invariant of the circumference.

12
